{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\kerning2\fs24 The Finance Director stated this evening we are here for the final portion of the FY 25-26 adopted millage and final budget. He went over the summary of the budget where the City transpired from our first public hearing to this evening. He went over historical millage and taxable value here within the City. He went over how the City went from the tentative budget to the final budget to the final budget plus prior year rollover. \par
\par
The Mayor opened the public hearing, and residents addressed the City Commission.\par
\par
Commissioner Howington stated last week she requested the Commission consider allowing her to give back something from her district. She handed out and explained a proposal that she created which could get the City to full rollback, and she requested that the Commission consider it.\par
\par
The Finance Director stated the Fiscal Year (FY) 2026 Budget commences on October 1, 2025, and continues through September 30, 2026, for the City of Deltona. The City Commission shall discuss the final millage necessary to fund the FY2025-2026 final budget for the City of Deltona. The City of Deltona is the taxing authority and the current year taxable value for operating purposes is $5,671,597,678. The adopted final millage rate of 6.7000 mills per $1,000.00 of valuation is a 2.30% increase in ad valorem tax revenues above the rolled back rate of $6.5494. The increase in millage over the rolled back rate is for funding of the operations, debt service, capital outlay and capital improvement programs of the City of Deltona. Major changes from last year are as follows: AHAC - $343,881; General Fund Debt Reduction - $333,529; Fraud and Compliance Audit - $500,000; Additional Litigation expenses - $1,000,000; 2 Additional Code Officers - $149,532; 2 Additional Vehicles for above officers - $72,000; 6 Additional Firefighters (75% of Fiscal Year) - $534,187; and Sheriff's Contract increases by $1.15 million. We did, however, have some reductions. We reduced the overall open City positions by 12 positions which is a reduction of $1 million citywide with a savings of $772,284 to the General Fund. Capital Improvements funded by the General Fund are as follows: Land Acquisition for Repetitive Loss Properties - $1 million; Fire Training Complex - $250,000; Parks Capital Improvements - $4.25 million; and Catalina Blvd & Elkcam Blvd Elevation - $1.04 million. All capital improvements and capital outlay for the current proposed fiscal year are funded by reserves. This budget includes all operating and capital costs for FY 2025-2026. This budget includes the General Special Revenue, Debt Service, Capital Enterprise and Internal Service Funds. The final citywide FY 2025-2026 budget for all funds is $245,510,105 and this resolution also includes rollover funds previously adopted of $99,153,709 bringing the total of all funds budget for FY 2025-2026 to $344,663,814. The City Commission adopts the citywide budget per the Charter and ordinance at the fund level.\cf1\kerning0\par
}

