{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Commissioner Santiago stated this is a discussion of removal of a Planning & Zoning (P&Z) Board Member. She wanted to start by saying that we have freedom of speech. We have the right to express our opinions, disagree with others, and post our thoughts on social media. But freedom of speech does not mean freedom from consequences. She had a concern recently that was raised regarding being disrespectful and inappropriate comments reportedly made by Mr. Blodgett, a member of the P&Z Board, towards a female resident. Let me be clear, this is not about silencing anyone or taking away their freedom of speech. This is about respect, professionalism, and accountability. Serving on a City board is a privilege, and with that privilege comes responsibility. Our residents deserve representatives who demonstrate respect, integrity, and good judgment.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\fs24 The Mayor opened public comments and residents addressed the City Commission.\par
\par
The Commission discussed the action, being a City representative, due process, accountability and extenuating circumstances.\par
\par
The City Attorney stated he wanted to provide some clarity. So under the Deltona City Code, the Planning and Zoning Board members all serve at the pleasure of the City Commission, full stop. The Florida statute mentioned earlier, Section One 12.501, which is entitled "Municipal Board Members Suspension Removal" states that for the purposes of this section, the term municipal board member is defined as any person who is appointed or confirmed by the governing body of a municipality to be a member of a board, commission, authority, or council, which is created or authorized by general law, special act, or municipal charter. The Planning and Zoning Board is authorized pursuant to the code of ordinances and it is not contained within the charter. The statute later goes on to state that this section applies in the absence of a local provision of a charter provision. So, the members of your planning and zoning board serve at the Commission's pleasure. Certainly Section 112.501 is informative and could be a valid process if the Commission would like to follow that process that provides for a resolution that states the basis, and then there is a notice and opportunity to be heard. However, because your local board is not authorized by general law or special act, which are acts of the legislature or contain a municipal charter Section 112.501 is not a mandatory process for this specific case.\par
\par
The Commission discussed social media, volunteers, politics, accountability, basing decisions on and Facebook. \cf1\par
}

