{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\kerning2\f0\fs24 The City Attorney explained in general proportionate fair share and this agreement. So, the development is entitled to pay the portion of impact that it has on an existing infrastructure with deficiencies. In this case, the infrastructure is Helen Boulevard, which is a county-maintained roadway. The only reason that the City is a party to this agreement is because it is within the City of Deltona, but the City does not have impacted infrastructure that requires proportionate fair share. The City does have impacts for which the City charges impact fees, and those impact fees are referenced in this agreement that there is an outstanding balance due that must be paid by the applicant as well. Essentially, the infrastructure being impacted that is subject to the proportionate fair share is county infrastructure, so the City is not entitled to additional payment above the impact fees. Impact fees are generally paid at the time of building permit. It is his understanding that there may already be permits issued, and these would have to be paid before the issuance of a certificate of occupancy.\par
\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
